  <content>
    <section class="learning-objectives" id="sect-00001">
      <title>Learning Objectives</title>
      <para id="para-00001">By the end of this section, you will be able to</para>
        <list id="list-00001">
          <item>Describe the characteristics of cognition in infancy</item>
          <item>Identify changes in mental abilities across infancy</item>
          <item>Compare and contrast the concepts of intelligence, wisdom, and creativity</item>
        </list>
    </section>
    <para id="para-00002">
      Gerald finds it hard to take young, 18 month old twins Tamara and Tara to their infant playgroup. He is the only grandpa among the parents, and although the young moms try to make him feel welcome, he knows that his family is different. Being the twins’ legal guardian is exhausting as a single man, and he hoped that he would find comradery and support in the playgroup. Instead, he is constantly trying to prove himself  to others that he provides a stimulating enough environment and high quality infant care.
    </para>
    <para id="para-00005">
      The other parents have started discussing childcare options, with many of the twins’ playmates starting to attend center care or registered home care. Gerald is unfamiliar with the world of formal childcare, as he was taken care of more informally by his family members when he was young. On his limited income, he cannot afford extra help and plans to continue to provide family care for Tamara and Tara.
    </para>
    <para id="para-00006">
      Each week at playgroup, he notices the cultural differences between the other families and his. New trends such as baby slings, infant carriers, and co-sleeping are foreign to him, having been raised in a different era with padded cribs and infant walker machine. He is interested in the discussions of screen time and how this impacts toddler’s emotional awareness, especially as he grew up in a more device free time. Gerald is thinking a lot about the contextual factors that influence infant and toddler development, which is what we will discuss in this section. <link target-id="Table02_02_01"/> provides examples of short-term and long-term business decisions.
    </para>
    <table id="Table02_02_01" summary="Alt text placeholder">
      <title>Comparing Short-Term and Long-Term Business Decisions</title>
      <tgroup cols="2">
        <thead>
          <row>
            <entry>Short-Term Business Decisions</entry>
            <entry>Long-Term Business Decisions</entry>
          </row>
          </thead>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <entry><list id="list-00002"><item>Accepting a special production order</item></list></entry>
            <entry><list id="list-00003"><item>Buying new equipment versus remodelings old equipment</item></list></entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><list id="list-00004"><item>Here is the second bulleted item. It is much longer so we can see what happens when items in the list need to wrap onto a second or even a third or even a fourth line.</item></list></entry>
            <entry><list id="list-00005"><item>Here is the second bulleted item. It is much longer so we can see what happens when items in the list need to wrap onto a second or even a third or even a fourth line.</item></list></entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><list id="list-00006"><item>Here is the third bulleted item in the list.</item></list></entry>
            <entry><list id="list-00007"><item>ere is the third bulleted item in the list.<footnote id="foot-00001">This is a footnote. It should appear on the page. It derives from inside a table.</footnote></item></list></entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><list id="list-00008"><item>Further processing or refining a current product</item></list></entry>
            <entry><list id="list-00009"><item>Diversify by buying another business</item></list></entry>
