      <caption>Table caption goes here.</caption>
      </table>
      <section id="sect-00008">
        <title>[H2]Families</title>
        <para id="para-00009">
          Families come in lots of constellations with many combinations of caregivers and children. WW-test notes that the heading below should be centered. WW-test notes this is a numeric list.</para>
          <para class="centered-text" id="para-00010">Managerial Decision-Making Process</para>
        <list list-type="enumerated" number-style="arabic" id="list-00010">
          <item>Identify the objective or goal, which for a business is typically maximizing income or minimizing costs.</item>
          <item>Identify alternative courses of action that can achieve a goal or solve a problem that is hindering reaching a goal.</item>
          <item>Perform a comprehensive (incremental) differential analysis of potential solutions. This includes identifying all costs, benefits and other financial and qualitative effects.</item>
          <item>Decide, based upon the analysis, the best course of action.</item>
          <item>Review, analyze and evaluate the results of the decision.</item>
        </list>
        <para id="para-00011">
          The main component of family structure that impacts infant and toddler social and emotional development is the number of caregivers. Families with two caregivers, such as a mom and a dad, or an aunt and grandma, or any other combination of two adults tend to experience less stress than single parent households. This is due to <term id="term-00001">time strain</term> and <term id="term-00002">financial strain</term>. Financial strain is stress that is experienced by families to provide shelter, food, entertainment, and education to their children. Although any family can experience financial strain, households with two adults have the potential to have two incomes and more financial resources. In comparison, a single parent family may experience more financial strain due to one parental figure needing to balance childcare and working outside the home. Time strain is stress that families experience to provide adequate attention, comfort, instruction, and love to their children. Again, in a two parent household, there may be the option for parents to take turns and balance quality family time, whereas single parents are more likely to face intense time strain due to feeling overworked.
        </para>
        <section id="sect-00009">
          <title>[H3]Communities</title>
          <para id="para-00012">
            Luckily, families exist within larger communities. Single parent households who live in communal and family focused neighborhoods may be able to rely on other households for support. Single parent networks, or groups of single parents who rely on each other for babysitting, childminding, and errand-running can help to decrease each others’ financial and time strain. Single parent and nuclear families who live close to extended family members may also rely on these family members to provide childcare and support.
          </para>
          <para id="para-00013">
            The impact of families and wider communities on infant social development can be viewed through Uri Bronfenbrenner’s ecological model as seen in Figure_04_05_Ecological. According to this model, children are nested within a <term id="term-00003">microsystem</term>, which is the direct, regular people in their lives. Quality of infant interactions with parents, family members, and neighbors makes up the microsystem. In addition, the interactions between parents with each other, and quality of relationships among family members that infants see regularly are the components of the <term id="term-00004">mesosystem</term>. Parents who interact hostile or lovingly with each other in front of their infant make up this level of influence.
          </para>
          <para id="para-00014">
            Moving beyond family, the ecological model’s next level is the <term id="term-00005">exosystem</term> which refers to indirect influence through community norms, neighborhood trends, and extended family environment. Living in a neighborhood in which neighbors are friendly and supportive towards one another or merely strangers influences this level. In addition, neighborhood and community safety, activities, and resources are also components of the exosystem.
          </para>
          <para id="para-00015">
            Next is the <term id="term-00006">macrosystem</term> which describes broad influences of culture, such a geography, religion, nationality, and language. Living in a culture that observes democratic voting and elections as compared to a culture that is represented by an appointed monarchy, or in a culture that provides free health care versus one in which health care is costly to families are features of the macrosystem.
          </para>
          <para id="para-00016">
            Finally, there is the <term id="term-00007">chronosystem</term> which describes major historical events that happen during an infant’s life that will impact their development, such as being young during a global pandemic when many families worked from home or growing up in times of war or natural disaster. This can also include the invention of new technologies, like the radio, or the internet.
          </para>
        </section>
        <section id="sect-00010">
          <title>[H3]Socialization and Cultural Practices</title>
          <para id="para-00017">
            Infants and toddlers are active in their environment, influencing others with smiles and cries, building attachment and relationships with caregivers, and forming core components of their identity. Around the world, infants are learning to accomplish these milestones in many different contexts, and throughout many historical eras (see <link target-id="Figure12_04_milgram"/>).
          </para>
          <figure id="Figure12_04_milgram">
            <media alt="Alt text placeholder.">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Figure12_04_milgram.png"/>
            </media>
              <caption>Figure caption.</caption>
          </figure>
        </section>
        <section id="sect-00011">
          <title>[H3]Childcare Settings</title>
          <para id="para-00018">
            Before the 1980s in North America, many two parent households would have one parent stay home to care for their infants and toddlers. Starting in the 1980s, a growing percentage of two parent households became <term id="term-00008">dual income households</term> which meant that both caregivers worked outside the home and young infants were taken to childcare settings.
          </para>
          <para id="para-00019">
            Childcare includes many arrangements including family care in which children are brought to an extended family member’s home, or an extended family member visits the child’s home. In family care, infants and toddlers may be with other children or cousins or may receive one-on-one attention. There are also nanny services in which one adult visits the child’s home and provide care for them one-on-one or with their siblings. In both family care and nanny services, children receive the stability of a familiar caregiver who provides for them in a familiar environment.
          </para>
          <section id="sect-00012">
            <title>[H4]Home Care</title>
            <para id="para-00020">
              Childcare might also take place in another family’s home. One example is <term id="term-00009">home care</term>, also called dayhomes. In home care, another family welcomes children into their home and provides care for them during the daytime working hours. Home care can be regulated with providers having training and certification in early childhood education and being registered with a state or province. Home care providers might also be informal and unregulated; in these cases providers may or may not have certification or training. In home care, infants and toddlers interact with a few other children in a home-like environment. WW-test notes this was added to see what results from an H4 header.
            </para>
          </section>
          <section id="sect-00013">
            <title>[H4]Center Care</title>
            <para id="para-00021">
              Finally, childcare may also include <term id="term-00010">center care</term> in which commercial properties such as childcare centers, early learning centers, and preschools offer care during daytime working hours. Centre care is typically the most regulated with centers being registered with the state or province and receiving regular check and visits from government officials. Staff who work in center care typically must have certification in early childhood education. In center care, infants and toddlers are often divided into classroom groups with similar aged peers and receive care from a variety of staff members. WW-test notes this was added to see what results from an H4 header.
            </para>
            <note class="it-depends" id="note-00001">
              <title>What Type of Childcare is Best?</title>
              <para id="para-00022">
                With so many options for childcare, parents can be overwhelmed with navigating what is best for their family. Traditionally, many families relied on family care, so it feels familiar and a great way to improve family bonds and pass along cultural values. If families are able to have one caregiver at home, or have an extended relative provide care, then this can help children to have a sense of security and help to facilitate the development of their identity within their family. But sometimes, family care is not possible, due to parents needing to work, and extended relatives living far away. Also, sometimes family care for only children means that toddlers do not have the opportunity to interact with other children, which is important for social development.
              </para>
              <para id="para-00023">
                Children who are more nervous, quiet, or have developmental exceptionalities may benefit from receiving care in a home setting provided by another family. Home care typically has a small number of children (6 or less) who play together in a provider’s home. This can allow children to make close bonds with others without being overwhelmed by the stimulation of a larger commercialized center. This can also bring them more confidence in leaving home and a good stepping stone before starting formal education.
              </para>
              <para id="para-00026">
                Finally, center care is beneficial for families who need reliable care. Whereas family care and home care might not be available during evenings or on holidays, commercialized child care centers often have longer opening hours, and extra staff on call for illness or emergencies. Being registered and regulated by government agencies, center care is typically held to high standards and provides enrichment in cognitive, emotional, and physical stimulation. These centers often have to pass rigorous safety checks as well. However, large centers are known to have outbreaks of disease, from stomach bugs to coughs, colds, and even lice. For some children with health considerations or weakened immunity, large centers may not be the best option.
              </para>
            </note>
            <para id="para-00027">
              In the United States, 20% of Black Americans and 17% of Hispanic Americans live in poverty as compared to 8% of White Americans and 8% of Asian Americans. In addition to poverty, Black and Hispanic families face barriers such as employment discrimination, police harassment, and intergenerational trauma from systemic racism which can impact how families provide for infants and toddlers. A family who is worried about encountering racist police officers might be too anxious and stressed to attend to their children’s emotional needs, preferring instead to prioritize their physical safety.
            </para>
          </section>
        </section>
      </section>


   

    <section class="summary" id="sect-00014">
      <list id="list-00011">
        <item>Families come in many possible compositions and constellations, but what impacts infant outcomes the most is the number of caregivers, as this impacts financial and time strain</item>
        <item>Families are embedded within layers of cultural and community influence which can impact infant development</item>
        <item>Childcare options are varied, but high quality childcare does not negatively impact the emotional and social well-being of toddlers and children</item>
        <item>Childrearing practices vary across cultures and historical time eras and can impact what skills are fostered and when</item>
      </list>
    </section>

